  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{2}{5}\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}+2$ is defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly decreasing.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$",
  "worked_solution": "We first write $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3/2}+2.\n$$\n\nThe function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{3/2}-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={x}^{3/2}-2{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={x}^{1/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =(x-2)\\sqrt{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(x-2)\\sqrt{x}<0.\n$$\n\nThe expression $\\sqrt{x}$ is always positive. So, this reduces to\n\n$$\nx-2<0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & <0 \\\\ x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is restricted to $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $x\\in (0,2).$",
